    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">What the Refinance Process Actually Looks Like From Application to Closing</h1>

    <p class="mt-8">If you've made the decision to refinance and you're ready to move forward, knowing what the process actually looks like from start to finish makes it significantly less stressful and helps you avoid the delays that slow most refinances down. A refinance is in many ways simpler than a purchase, but it has its own friction points, and most of them are predictable.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Process in Plain Terms</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">A refinance replaces your existing mortgage with a new one. The new lender pays off your current loan, and you begin making payments on the new loan under the new terms. From your perspective, the process involves an application, an appraisal in most cases, underwriting, and a closing. From the lender's perspective, it involves verifying that you qualify for the new loan and that the property supports the new loan amount. Most refinances close in 30 to 45 days from application, though the timeline varies based on lender volume, appraisal scheduling, and how quickly documentation is submitted.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Hidden Friction: Where Refinances Actually Slow Down</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Here's where most refinance timelines extend beyond what borrowers expected. The three most common delay sources are documentation gaps, appraisal scheduling, and title issues.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Documentation gaps happen when borrowers submit incomplete files. A missing page from a bank statement, a tax return that doesn't include all schedules, a pay stub that's more than 30 days old by the time underwriting reviews it, each of those requires a follow-up request and a delay. Submitting a complete, organized document package at the start of the process is the single most effective way to keep a refinance on schedule.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Appraisal scheduling is outside your direct control but affects your timeline. In high-volume markets or busy seasons, appraisal appointments can take one to two weeks to schedule, and the report takes additional time after the appointment. Ordering the appraisal as early in the process as possible shortens that window.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Title issues surface when a title search reveals liens, judgments, or ownership discrepancies that need to be resolved before the new loan can close. Many title issues are resolvable but take time. Homeowners who are aware of any potential title complications should raise them with their lender early rather than waiting for them to appear in the title report.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Documents You'll Need</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Most refinances require a similar set of documentation to a purchase mortgage. For employed borrowers: two years of W-2s, your two most recent pay stubs, two months of bank statements for all accounts used for closing, and your most recent mortgage statement. For self-employed borrowers: two years of personal and business tax returns, a year-to-date profit and loss statement, and two months of business bank statements.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Beyond income and asset documentation, your lender will need your homeowner's insurance information and, if applicable, your HOA contact information for any required certifications. Having all of these organized before you apply is what keeps the process moving at the pace the lender is capable of.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Assemble your full documentation package before you submit your application.</strong> Two years of returns, recent pay stubs, two months of bank statements, your current mortgage statement, and your insurance information. A complete file at submission prevents the back-and-forth that extends timelines.</li>
      <li><strong>Lock your rate as soon as you have a complete application and you're comfortable with the pricing.</strong> Rate locks are typically available for 30 to 60 days. A lock that expires before closing requires an extension, which carries a cost. Lock early enough to give the process time to complete.</li>
      <li><strong>Respond to lender requests within 24 hours.</strong> Underwriting conditions move to the back of the processing queue when they sit unanswered. Fast responses keep your file moving.</li>
      <li><strong>Review your closing disclosure carefully before closing day.</strong> The closing disclosure shows your final loan terms, closing costs, and cash due at closing. Compare it to your loan estimate and ask your lender about any differences before you arrive at the table.</li>
      <li><strong>Be aware of the three-day rescission period on refinances.</strong> Unlike a purchase, a refinance on a primary residence includes a three-business-day right of rescission after closing during which you can cancel the transaction. Your new loan doesn't fund until that period expires. Factor that into any timeline expectations you've communicated to contractors or others depending on your access to funds.</li>
    </ol>

